From de0540026707f383ed89456b7d99290b38d2662e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 20:57:38 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] bufferplan.h -- the stall clock is not the wall clock

This file claimed a source working through netplan's retries could never
be cut off: the backoff table tops out at 15 s and
BUFPLAN_STALL_GIVEUP_MS is 30 s, so the arithmetic looked safe.

**The arithmetic left out how long an attempt takes.** On the board, a
resume from pause hit a server that completed the TLS handshake and then
timed out waiting for data -- `Connection timed out before data was
ready!`, about six seconds each, five times. The backoff never mattered.
The attempts did. The fifth one connected and got `HTTP 200 -> play`,
and the stall rule had given up 25 ms earlier:

  attempt 4 failed after 5936 ms; retrying in 2064 ms
  hop 2: HTTP 200 -> play, connect+TLS 1739 ms
  only 0 audio bytes before the drop
  stream ended: ended, 0 rebuffers, 30025 ms silent, 1214 frames

30025 against a 30000 ms limit. The stream was killed at the moment it
succeeded, and every line of the log reads as the station hanging up when
what hung up was this rule -- the same way 0203's read as a station
hanging up when the player was hanging up on itself.

So the clock has to measure the right silence. A stream rebuffering
behind a source doing nothing should end; a stream rebuffering behind a
source that is opening sockets and completing handshakes is getting
somewhere, and the phase timer cannot see the difference because nothing
it is given moves.

bufplan_note_attempt() restarts it, and the caller notices on the EDGE of
netstream_failures() -- a count that rises once per failed attempt and
resets when audio flows, so both directions are news. Not the state:
that sits in CONNECTING for six seconds at a time and would never produce
an edge. Only in PREROLL and REBUFFERING; in PLAYING the clock is being
used for something else and a note must not move it, which is asserted.

The rule still bites. Thirty seconds after the LAST attempt, rather than
after the first -- which is what it was always meant to say, and is the
case a genuinely dead source still falls into.

12 new host checks, and the loop is written to actually cross 30 s of
wall clock with an assertion that it did, so the test cannot pass by
failing to reproduce the fault. 20165 checks, 0 failures.

Not flashed. The test is a pause and a resume on WNZK, which is where it
came from -- zeno.fm 302s to surfernetwork and is slow and flaky enough
to reproduce it, which makes it worth keeping in the station list.
