-+From f6412187e874d1a21d98ce5b2950838034b3e01b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
-+From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
-+Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 20:31:06 +0000
-+Subject: [PATCH 306/307] radiobrowser.h -- the request, and proof the M3U bet
-+ holds
-+
-+The README has named radio-browser.info as where station data comes from
-+since v0.4.0 was scoped, and until this patch not one line of code
-+mentioned it. Everything in the stream path was chosen for it --
-+stations.m3u is M3U *because* radio-browser serves M3U, and
-+stationlist.h says exactly that in its first paragraph -- and the bet was
-+never checked against the server's real output.
-+
-+So this is the first step and it is the half a host can finish: the URL,
-+the headers, the limits, and a texttest. No socket, no task, nothing that
-+needs a board. Same order stationlist.h went in before phases 2 and 3,
-+and for the same reason.
-+
-+THE BET HOLDS. The server's M3U is not the file stations.m3u.example
-+shows -- it carries #RADIOBROWSERUUID before each #EXTINF, and a blank
-+line between entries. stationlist_parse() reads it correctly, and by rule
-+rather than by luck: the clause that skips every # line that is not
-+#EXTINF was written for files that have been through another player, and
-+this is that case. The fixture in radiobrowsertest.c is the documented
-+response shape and it parses to four stations with the right names,
-+including a comma in one. Had that failed, two headers and a format
-+decision would have failed with it.
-+
-+Four API facts that are not obvious and each of which would have cost a
-+flash to learn:
-+
-+**The pool name cannot be used over TLS.** all.api.radio-browser.info is
-+the documented way to find a mirror, and it is a DNS construct rather
-+than a server, so it has no certificate valid for that name. This player
-+verifies certificates and is not going to stop in order to reach a
-+station directory. Hence a short hardcoded list of named mirrors, which
-+is the thing the DNS lookup exists to avoid -- accepted deliberately,
-+with the exit written down: the proper fix is the SRV record,
-+esp_netif has no SRV resolver, and adding one is bigger than the feature.
-+
-+**The search endpoint is /m3u/stations/search?name=, not
-+/m3u/stations/byname/{term}.** Both exist. The term is a path segment in
-+the second, and an encoded slash in a path segment is rewritten or
-+rejected by enough intermediaries that it is not worth finding out which.
-+
-+**The service asks for three things** and all are cheap: a descriptive
-+appname/appversion User-Agent, at most 2-3 requests a second with results
-+cached 5-15 minutes, and a call to /json/url/{uuid} when a listener
-+starts a stream so the directory can count what is actually played. That
-+last is the only thing asked in return for having no key and no account,
-+and it needs the UUID -- which is only in the response. So a stations.m3u
-+written from a search must copy the comment lines through verbatim rather
-+than being regenerated from parsed stations. That is written at the top
-+of the header, where whoever adds the writing will be.
-+
-+**The default limit is 100000**, which would be a 40 MB M3U into a 64 KB
-+buffer. 50, with a check that it stays under STATIONLIST_MAX.
-+
-+The encoder is the part with real input: a term typed into a form can
-+contain &, =, #, +, %, a space or UTF-8, and + is the nasty one because
-+it is legal in a path and means a space in a query. Unreserved is RFC
-+3986's set and nothing wider -- encoding a character that did not need it
-+costs two bytes, and not encoding one that did is a different request
-+from the one the listener asked for. Every refusal empties its output
-+rather than truncating, because half a percent-escape is not a shorter
-+search.
-+
-+65 checks, 0 failures, under ASan and UBSan, and compiled once more at
-+-O2 -Werror. Nothing here has been through the cross compiler and nothing
-+calls it yet: this is the request, not the fetch.
