+--Subject: [PATCH 300/303] player.c -- a stream's text rows are the stream's
+--
+--play_stream()'s list of "the parts a stream does need" retires the
+--previous track's numbers and its visuals and does not retire its text.
+--ui_task reads s_tags_shown and s_name_shown, both copies taken only when
+--s_text_staged is set, and it prefers s_tags_shown.title over the name
+--whenever that title is non-empty. So the station name went into
+--s_display_name, nothing staged it, and the screen kept showing the last
+--FILE's title, artist and album -- not for a moment, for the whole
+--broadcast.
+--
+--CLAUDE.md's open list records this as "the screen shows only the station
+--name", which is what every run so far saw, and the reason is that each of
+--them chose a station from a boot that had played nothing: s_tags_shown is
+--zeroed then, so the fallback to s_name_shown is accidentally correct. The
+--fault needs a file played first, which is the ordinary route to the RADIO
+--tab and the one order not yet flashed.
+--
+--Staged through the existing mechanism rather than written straight into
+--the _shown copies, because those belong to ui_task; this is the same
+--publish-a-value rule s_ring_pct follows. s_text_release is set with it so
+--the copy is taken on the next pass instead of after TEXT_HOLD_MS -- that
+--hold exists to land text and cover art in one frame, and ui_clear_art()
+--two lines above has said there is no cover coming. s_text_staged is
+--written last so ui_task cannot sample a station name beside a file's
+--artist.
+--
+--One consequence is worth having written down where it can be read: after
+--this, s_name_shown is the s_stream_top pointer, so the later in-place
+--rewrite of the top line -- a station's own icy-name displacing the one the
+--list gave -- reaches the screen with no second stage. That branch gets a
+--comment saying so, and saying why s_stream_top cannot become a local.
+--
+--Nothing drawn changes for a file. Board-testable in one step: play a
+--tagged track, open RADIO, pick a station, and read the three text rows.
