++++ b/out/0309-netdec.c-a-web-page-is-a-verdict-not-a-delay.patch
+@@ -0,0 +1,162 @@
++From 9f8deb017d8442ceaaa85dbfea3f23eade3c33f0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
++From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
++Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 20:44:48 +0000
++Subject: [PATCH 309/309] netdec.c -- a web page is a verdict, not a delay
++
++The BBC URL in the test list 302s to https://www.bbc.co.uk/ and serves
++HTML. netstream detected it correctly at the header --
++`first audio bytes look like HTML (content-type text/html)` -- went
++buffering -> playing anyway, and then netdec printed
++
++  W tab5_netdec: The station returned a web page
++
++at the decode loop's rate for thirteen seconds. About 1300 lines, which
++is the entire log of that run, and it stopped only because the listener
++pressed something. `stream ended: ended, 0 rebuffers, 13126 ms silent,
++0 frames`.
++
++TWO BUGS, ONE CAUSE, AND THE CAUSE IS THE SERIES' FAVOURITE.
++STREAM_CODEC_NONE means two things and the enum cannot tell them apart:
++"not identified yet" before the sniff, and "identified, and it is a web
++page" after it. identify() opens with `if (s_codec != NONE) return;`
++using NONE as "keep trying", and then writes NONE for a permanent
++verdict. So the verdict was re-reached, and re-announced, on every call.
++
++This is 0203's NETSTREAM_IDLE and 0209's stations_load() return value
++for the third time: a value asked a question it does not answer. The fix
++is the same shape -- a separate latch, s_not_audio, set where the verdict
++is reached and cleared only by open and close. Not by
++netdec_reconnect(), deliberately: a station that served HTML will serve
++HTML again, and the codec is meant to survive a reconnect anyway.
++
++The second bug is the comment that was there, which said sniffing and
++not-audio are the same to the caller and both mean wait. They are not.
++One ends; the other is a socket held open to a web server feeding a
++decoder that will never produce a sample. netdec_read() now returns -1,
++which is what the unsupported-codec branch below it already returns and
++what bufferplan reads as the source being finished -- so play_stream()
++tears down, logs why, and streamplan_status() has "No signal" for the
++screen. Which is, at last, true.
++
++Reached by the same verdict and therefore also fixed: a link to an .m3u
++or .pls (`That link is a playlist, not a stream`), and Ogg or FLAC
++streams, which are refused on purpose. All three previously spun.
++
++Not flashed. The station in the list that caused it is left in place --
++a URL that 302s to a web page is an ordinary thing for a station list to
++contain, and it is now the test case for this.
++---
